#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151842 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152095 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f567e86-63a5-512d-b830-be97bedcf0c8" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Second Division Property"}] :question/id #uuid "c638e469-4c15-52f3-a397-a7aac86d654e" :question/math-academy-id "e-14588" :question/problem "If $ka \\equiv kb \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\,7)$\nis equivalent to $%\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\na \\equiv b \\:(\\textrm{mod} \\,7),$\nwhich of the following could be the value of $k?$\n\n- $k=-12$\n\n- $k=4$\n\n- $k=-14$" :question/worked-solution "The division property of modular arithmetic states that\n\n$$\nka\\equiv kb\\quad(\\textrm{mod} \\,n) \\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow\\qquad a \\equiv b \\quad(\\textrm{mod}\\, n)\n$$\n\nprovided that $k$ and $n$ are coprime. In other words, we require $\\text{gcd}(k,n)=1.$\n\nIn this case, the modulus $n=7.$ Let's consider each value of $k$ in turn.\n\n- If $k=-12,$ then $\\text{gcd}(k,n) = \\text{gcd}(-12,7) = 1.$ Therefore, our equivalence is valid for $k=-12.$\n\n- If $k=4,$ then $\\text{gcd}(k,n) = \\text{gcd}(4,7) = 1.$ Therefore, our equivalence is valid for $k=4.$\n\n- If $k=-14,$ then $\\text{gcd}(k,n) = \\text{gcd}(-14,7) = 7\\neq 1.$ Therefore, our equivalence is *not* valid for $k=-14.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151843 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152094 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bca70f6c-d087-5b8e-aff2-581adca9ed1f" :knowledge-point/title "Using the First Division Property"}] :question/id #uuid "9f2134e8-62dd-5748-a202-819b4ca5208f" :question/math-academy-id "e-14589" :question/problem "Given that $4a \\equiv 4b \\,(\\textrm{mod}\\,8),$ which of the following statements **must** be true?\n\n- $a \\equiv b \\quad(\\textrm{mod}\\,4)$\n\n- $a \\equiv b \\quad(\\textrm{mod}\\,2)$\n\n- $a \\equiv b \\quad(\\textrm{mod}\\,8)$" :question/worked-solution "The division property of modular arithmetic states that for $k\\neq 0,$ we have\n\n$$\nka\\equiv kb\\quad(\\textrm{mod} \\,kn) \\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow\\qquad a \\equiv b \\quad(\\textrm{mod}\\, n).\n$$\n\nWe're asked to consider the following statement:\n\n$$\n4a \\equiv 4b \\quad(\\textrm{mod}\\,8)\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\text{gcd}(4,8) =4.$\n\nApplying the division property, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4a & ≡4b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,8) \\\\ 4\\cdot a & ≡4\\cdot b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,4\\cdot 2) \\\\ \\cancel{4}\\cdot a & ≡\\cancel{4}\\cdot b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,\\cancel{4}\\cdot 2) \\\\ a & ≡b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151858 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152096 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3822141e-3cea-5c34-b925-a522c2273335" :knowledge-point/title "Combining the Division Properties"}] :question/id #uuid "344211e7-11c0-54f9-8a78-e64ff53deff0" :question/math-academy-id "e-14612" :question/problem "Given that $15a \\equiv 15b \\,(\\textrm{mod}\\,20),$ which of the following statements **must** be true?\n\n- $a \\equiv b \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\, 20)$\n\n- $a \\equiv b \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,4)$\n\n- $7a \\equiv 7b \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,4)$" :question/worked-solution "The division properties of modular arithmetic state the following:\n\n- For $k\\neq 0,$ we have\n\n$$\nka \\equiv kb \\quad(\\text{mod}\\,kn) \\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow \\qquad a \\equiv b \\quad(\\textrm{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\n- For $\\text{gcd}(k, n) = 1,$ we have \n\n$$\nka \\equiv kb \\quad(\\text{mod}\\,n) \\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow \\qquad a \\equiv b \\quad(\\textrm{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. Applying the first property, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15a & ≡15b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,20) \\\\ 5\\cdot 3\\cdot a & ≡5\\cdot 3\\cdot b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,5\\cdot 4) \\\\ \\cancel{5}\\cdot 3\\cdot a & ≡\\cancel{5}\\cdot 3\\cdot b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,\\cancel{5}\\cdot 4) \\\\ 3a & ≡3b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\gcd(3,4)=1,$ by the second property, we have:\n\n$$\n3a \\equiv 3b \\quad(\\textrm{mod}\\,4) \\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow \\qquad a \\equiv b \\quad(\\textrm{mod}\\,4)\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. By statement II and the second property, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,4)\\,⟺\\,7a≡7b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415053 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152095 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f567e86-63a5-512d-b830-be97bedcf0c8" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Second Division Property"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d32cab0-a947-5df3-b52a-1e20d4a89706" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414933} {:answer/id #uuid "19a4b986-70e3-5216-b16f-03a5fda6657d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414939} {:answer/id #uuid "5a04721a-ae8a-5b6e-8878-aabea63fdaaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414956} {:answer/id #uuid "636fa707-191d-5267-8fee-440385919afb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414961} {:answer/id #uuid "a4b3e191-4f8f-5513-9308-e68abbfe8bcb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414987}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a4b3e191-4f8f-5513-9308-e68abbfe8bcb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414987} :answer-field/id #uuid "68b518f5-1d9e-5140-a83d-d1e076453bbe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415029}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "eaf9f361-8af6-5a67-abf5-86a0bbac7a1d" :question/math-academy-id "q-103018" :question/problem "If \n$ka≡kb\\,(\\text{mod}\\,10)$\nis equivalent to\n$a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,10),$\nwhich of the following could be the value of $k?$\n\n- $k=12$\n- $k=3$\n- $k=-5$" :question/worked-solution "The division property of modular arithmetic states that\n\n$$\nka≡kb\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\n$$\n\nprovided that $k$ and $n$ are coprime. In other words, we require $\\text{gcd}(k,n)=1.$\n\nIn this case, the modulus $n=10.$ Let's consider each value of $k$ in turn.\n\n- If $k=12,$ then $\\text{gcd}(k,n)=\\text{gcd}(12,10)=2\\ne 1.$ Therefore, our equivalence is *not* valid for $k=12.$\n\n- If $k=3,$ then $\\text{gcd}(k,n)=\\text{gcd}(3,10)=1.$ Therefore, our equivalence is valid for $k=3.$\n\n- If $k=-5,$ then $\\text{gcd}(k,n)=\\text{gcd}(-5,10)=5\\ne 1.$ Therefore, our equivalence is *not* valid for $k=-5.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415050 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152095 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f567e86-63a5-512d-b830-be97bedcf0c8" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Second Division Property"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ab1a91d-d33a-5e51-b220-e0e4da05d420" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414931} {:answer/id #uuid "a0f6d41d-dc0c-54e9-864e-febae8295631" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414985} {:answer/id #uuid "a3d03008-076d-5979-8ec2-d15997b802f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414986} {:answer/id #uuid "ee51c39b-f541-5db9-9b1d-bc0125830007" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415009} {:answer/id #uuid "f95294ea-d39c-5f5e-8043-8a30f497210f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415014}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a0f6d41d-dc0c-54e9-864e-febae8295631" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414985} :answer-field/id #uuid "185bfed6-c6e9-5751-adee-92e27148dc54" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415021}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "cdd37720-ffc4-552d-8b81-f3aa2f866efb" :question/math-academy-id "q-103026" :question/problem "If \n$ka≡kb\\,(\\text{mod}\\,8)$\nis equivalent to\n$a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,8),$\nwhich of the following could be the value of $k?$\n\n- $k=8$\n- $k=-12$\n- $k=-15$" :question/worked-solution "The division property of modular arithmetic states that\n\n$$\nka≡kb\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\n$$\n\nprovided that $k$ and $n$ are coprime. In other words, we require $\\text{gcd}(k,n)=1.$\n\nIn this case, the modulus $n=8.$ Let's consider each value of $k$ in turn.\n\n- If $k=8,$ then $\\text{gcd}(k,n)=\\text{gcd}(8,8)=8\\ne 1.$ Therefore, our equivalence is *not* valid for $k=8.$\n\n- If $k=-12,$ then $\\text{gcd}(k,n)=\\text{gcd}(-12,8)=4\\ne 1.$ Therefore, our equivalence is *not* valid for $k=-12.$\n\n- If $k=-15,$ then $\\text{gcd}(k,n)=\\text{gcd}(-15,8)=1.$ Therefore, our equivalence is valid for $k=-15.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415051 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152094 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bca70f6c-d087-5b8e-aff2-581adca9ed1f" :knowledge-point/title "Using the First Division Property"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ad3b1aa-93e7-5b73-a4b4-ec7fb6b5d2f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414948} {:answer/id #uuid "9b4bf61e-6b83-5157-b763-8513937c1774" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414983} {:answer/id #uuid "be01c4c9-9b6c-5fb4-b2ec-7c85edac2cfd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414996} {:answer/id #uuid "e0a31f1f-fd54-58eb-b1f3-5e9c47ac9da1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415006} {:answer/id #uuid "e7f12825-c2b7-5186-8218-7c940c5283cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415007}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e7f12825-c2b7-5186-8218-7c940c5283cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415007} :answer-field/id #uuid "5139e167-eff8-5fb2-95ba-ff706a30c921" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415026}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d23e3ed0-5575-5880-b9bf-7ff1adfe9936" :question/math-academy-id "q-103168" :question/problem "Given that $3a≡3b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,12),$ which of the following statements **must** be true?\n\n- $a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3)$\n- $a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,4)$\n- $a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6)$" :question/worked-solution "The division property of modular arithmetic states that for $k\\ne 0,$ we have\n\n$$\nka≡kb\\,(\\text{mod}\\,kn)\\,⟺\\,a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nWe're asked to consider the following statement:\n\n$$\n3a≡3b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,12)\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\text{gcd}(3,12)=3.$\n\nApplying the division property, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3a & ≡3b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,12) \\\\ 3\\cdot a & ≡3\\cdot b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,3\\cdot 4) \\\\ \\cancel{3}\\cdot a & ≡\\cancel{3}\\cdot b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,\\cancel{3}\\cdot 4) \\\\ a & ≡b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415047 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152094 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bca70f6c-d087-5b8e-aff2-581adca9ed1f" :knowledge-point/title "Using the First Division Property"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43a7e751-1cfd-5fee-b159-3b5f2b487bc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414951} {:answer/id #uuid "69bb5ea8-a037-5146-805c-1dd1ecec0c01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414962} {:answer/id #uuid "6e9f8253-0b3f-5665-a130-2f4bb45db984" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414965} {:answer/id #uuid "9a6c4a6b-24ed-570f-8486-7fc8b14b4ff9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414982} {:answer/id #uuid "f51e8872-95d1-53a4-be14-a0d7848bcba9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415012}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f51e8872-95d1-53a4-be14-a0d7848bcba9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415012} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc93f710-a95a-5049-bcda-b4e2876fa001" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415040}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c4019e4a-38d9-5f9b-be44-5c0d23af5290" :question/math-academy-id "q-103171" :question/problem "Given that $2a≡2b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,8),$ which of the following statements **must** be true?\n\n- $a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6)$\n- $a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,4)$\n- $a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,8)$" :question/worked-solution "The division property of modular arithmetic states that for $k\\ne 0,$ we have\n\n$$\nka≡kb\\,(\\text{mod}\\,kn)\\,⟺\\,a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nWe're asked to consider the following statement:\n\n$$\n2a≡2b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,8)\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\text{gcd}(2,8)=2.$\n\nApplying the division property, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2a & ≡2b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,8) \\\\ 2\\cdot a & ≡2\\cdot b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,2\\cdot 4) \\\\ \\cancel{2}\\cdot a & ≡\\cancel{2}\\cdot b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,\\cancel{2}\\cdot 4) \\\\ a & ≡b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415043 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152094 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bca70f6c-d087-5b8e-aff2-581adca9ed1f" :knowledge-point/title "Using the First Division Property"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b40d7ae-81fd-5560-a3b6-1912b4d03c01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414944} {:answer/id #uuid "6333918e-5906-5548-8e59-11cfdafb53e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414960} {:answer/id #uuid "b5c3b253-f21f-58e1-98dc-54b06420dd03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414992} {:answer/id #uuid "dd07f6b5-d64e-5f75-85b5-a565d52ddc05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415003} {:answer/id #uuid "ffd323a5-6eca-50fb-aef2-a5207b006ddf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415017}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b5c3b253-f21f-58e1-98dc-54b06420dd03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414992} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac14ead2-1a03-5fa3-95d0-5a72490e2583" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415034}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0ff193af-a6fe-56c6-94b7-27cf32c1ffe5" :question/math-academy-id "q-103191" :question/problem "Given that $6a≡6b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,9),$ which of the following statements **must** be true?\n\n- $2a≡2b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3)$\n- $3a≡3b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6)$\n- $a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,9)$" :question/worked-solution "The division property of modular arithmetic states that for $k\\ne 0,$ we have\n\n$$\nka≡kb\\,(\\text{mod}\\,kn)\\,⟺\\,a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nWe're asked to consider the following statement:\n\n$$\n6a≡6b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,9)\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\text{gcd}(6,9)=3.$\n\nApplying the division property, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6a & ≡6b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,9) \\\\ 3\\cdot 2\\cdot a & ≡3\\cdot 2\\cdot b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,3\\cdot 3) \\\\ \\cancel{3}\\cdot 2\\cdot a & ≡\\cancel{3}\\cdot 2\\cdot b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,\\cancel{3}\\cdot 3) \\\\ 2a & ≡2b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152477 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152095 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f567e86-63a5-512d-b830-be97bedcf0c8" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Second Division Property"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d2b6db7-48a7-5c5f-8a77-2fe48526a608" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "is not equivalent to" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414932} {:answer/id #uuid "3762f2de-c583-5714-9b48-7c02a54eec06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "is equivalent to" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414947}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3762f2de-c583-5714-9b48-7c02a54eec06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "is equivalent to" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414947} :answer-field/id #uuid "089f9845-9ec3-5dd6-82b2-e24dceac91c9" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415018} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "71813022-7564-5bad-a001-0b629b968f8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414966} {:answer/id #uuid "811453d5-8d65-549e-b3e4-1acc0e2fcabb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414970}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "811453d5-8d65-549e-b3e4-1acc0e2fcabb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414970} :answer-field/id #uuid "10a0a6ca-8535-5d29-a558-33adedbec76b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415020} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17fc27ca-e10f-52c0-9489-6344d9f26ee7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "positive" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414938} {:answer/id #uuid "22d78d51-9cf3-571e-ad19-abf789362266" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "prime" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414941} {:answer/id #uuid "a0a30d16-8e5e-5020-a255-31f75e467803" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "coprime" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414984} {:answer/id #uuid "b97d8b88-8c98-51c9-978a-4e3d68460458" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "not prime" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414993} {:answer/id #uuid "da0f3a7a-8b14-53b9-8811-33d8e99b6659" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "not coprime" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415002}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a0a30d16-8e5e-5020-a255-31f75e467803" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "coprime" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414984} :answer-field/id #uuid "451eb193-baa8-57c7-bc96-4a6c94ae27d0" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415024}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "826a8410-28c1-5981-bb78-74772400c329" :question/math-academy-id "q-212053" :question/problem "Consider the following congruence:\n\n$$\n5\\cdot 2a≡5\\cdot 2b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,18)\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The division property of modular arithmetic states that\n\n$$\nka≡kb\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\n$$\n\nprovided that $k$ and $n$ are coprime. In other words, we require $\\text{gcd}(k,n)=1.$\n\nIn our case, the factor $k=5$ and the modulus $n=18$ are coprime since $\\text{gcd}(5,18)=1.$ As a result, we can cancel out $5$ from both sides of the congruence:\n\n$$\n\\cancel{5}\\cdot 2a≡\\cancel{5}\\cdot 2b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,18)\\,⟺\\,2a≡2b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,18)\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following: \n\nSince $5$ and $18$ are $\\text{coprime},$ this congruence $\\text{is equivalent to}$ $2a≡2b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,18).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152478 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152095 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f567e86-63a5-512d-b830-be97bedcf0c8" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Second Division Property"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06f2ac01-f5af-5965-86e1-c7f62856aeee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "coprime" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414929} {:answer/id #uuid "1bda5ed8-d8cc-50c7-9331-e4fef732089e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "not prime" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414940} {:answer/id #uuid "2c4be225-a4d9-531c-b0d6-2d3379f49d80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "not coprime" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414945} {:answer/id #uuid "5e56c4c8-f198-52b5-8616-bfa70a20388d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "prime" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414958} {:answer/id #uuid "f383414e-73d6-529e-81fc-27a1cd96901f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "positive" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415011}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2c4be225-a4d9-531c-b0d6-2d3379f49d80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "not coprime" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414945} :answer-field/id #uuid "5232d044-7444-501b-9f80-621e603355e7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415027} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d6043cc0-a7de-5794-b7fd-424efd9bf2be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "is equivalent to" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415000} {:answer/id #uuid "df0bcfc7-783b-55cf-ae13-d6891efbd370" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "is not equivalent to" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415004}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "df0bcfc7-783b-55cf-ae13-d6891efbd370" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "is not equivalent to" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415004} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5ba1118-d0b5-57b8-9b1b-d30adecd4205" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415039}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4dbb180b-acf0-5684-81e4-b820ca5ce13c" :question/math-academy-id "q-212054" :question/problem "Consider the following congruence:\n\n$$\n2\\cdot 3a≡2\\cdot 3b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,16)\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The division property of modular arithmetic states that\n\n$$\nka≡kb\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\n$$\n\nprovided that $k$ and $n$ are coprime. In other words, we require $\\text{gcd}(k,n)=1.$\n\nIn our case, the factor $k=2$ and the modulus $n=16$ are not coprime since $\\text{gcd}(2,16)=2.$ As a result, we cannot cancel out $2$ from both sides of the congruence.\n\nTherefore, we have the following: \n\nSince $2$ and $16$ are $\\text{not coprime},$ this congruence $\\text{is not equivalent to}$ $3a≡3b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,16).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415052 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152094 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bca70f6c-d087-5b8e-aff2-581adca9ed1f" :knowledge-point/title "Using the First Division Property"}] :quest