3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414955} {:answer/id #uuid "980b9d16-a277-5c67-ac99-eca4db31e201" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414980}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "545c6db1-d0af-5209-bb48-87511b413039" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414955} :answer-field/id #uuid "83f59b7b-ab84-5670-839c-3443e598aed3" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415031}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c54d6f9f-c7e1-5ec9-825f-3e37025371fc" :question/math-academy-id "q-213274" :question/problem "Given that $9a≡9b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,15),$ construct a statement that also must be true:" :question/worked-solution "The division property of modular arithmetic states that for $k\\ne 0,$ we have\n\n$$\nka≡kb\\,(\\text{mod}\\,kn)\\,⟺\\,a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have a common factor of $k=3,$ and the congruence can be written as\n\n$$\n3\\cdot 3a≡3\\cdot 3b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3\\cdot 5).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can cancel out the common factor on both sides of the congruence and the modulus:\n\n$$\n\\cancel{3}\\cdot 3a≡\\cancel{3}\\cdot 3b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,\\cancel{3}\\cdot 5)\\,⟺\\,3a≡3b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,5)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415042 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152095 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f567e86-63a5-512d-b830-be97bedcf0c8" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Second Division Property"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29196106-11af-548e-bfc3-e6704c15ba7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "is equivalent to" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414943} {:answer/id #uuid "81f2f006-98fc-5ac6-9066-872370d0cc62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "is not equivalent to" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414973}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "81f2f006-98fc-5ac6-9066-872370d0cc62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "is not equivalent to" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414973} :answer-field/id #uuid "4a196bd6-fe7f-5fb1-8c2b-77609a70a373" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415025} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d52c292-fdaf-50b0-9c28-3b056bd0cb36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "not coprime" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414934} {:answer/id #uuid "10610c54-ff51-5307-9f90-b6eabfb1e4a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "prime" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414936} {:answer/id #uuid "6e494e63-dbeb-5d49-9517-6889039cbebf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "coprime" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414964} {:answer/id #uuid "837f43b2-0cf6-5314-9737-f6c3b2f8c816" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "not prime" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414974} {:answer/id #uuid "ce855e48-d462-5bcb-99cf-ba99969f3e21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "positive" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414998}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0d52c292-fdaf-50b0-9c28-3b056bd0cb36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "not coprime" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414934} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc4a4f00-eeac-5e19-81e7-8f21d410c0e3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415038}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0db74dcd-d670-5d6c-b7fa-69c8b873b4a0" :question/math-academy-id "q-213286" :question/problem "Consider the following congruence:\n\n$$\n5\\cdot 2a≡5\\cdot 2b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,10)\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The division property of modular arithmetic states that\n\n$$\nka≡kb\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\n$$\n\nprovided that $k$ and $n$ are coprime. In other words, we require $\\text{gcd}(k,n)=1.$\n\nIn our case, the factor $k=5$ and the modulus $n=10$ are not coprime since $\\text{gcd}(5,10)=5.$ As a result, we cannot cancel out $5$ from both sides of the congruence.\n\nTherefore, we have the following: \n\nSince $5$ and $10$ are $\\text{not coprime},$ this congruence $\\text{is not equivalent to}$ $2a≡2b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,10).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415045 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152096 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3822141e-3cea-5c34-b925-a522c2273335" :knowledge-point/title "Combining the Division Properties"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "165e395d-05d0-5648-8b98-661d9a74d37b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414937} {:answer/id #uuid "533aa886-6946-531f-bdb2-e7a060c00f71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414953} {:answer/id #uuid "5d847c60-18f9-5cd0-bc60-3e38b022ff75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414957} {:answer/id #uuid "6072926a-de75-570c-be5c-7b19ca2fa4ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414959} {:answer/id #uuid "76724f64-2846-5362-a726-6c451b64e577" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414968}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6072926a-de75-570c-be5c-7b19ca2fa4ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414959} :answer-field/id #uuid "b17312d2-4961-5057-924d-78f6ff17fb06" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415035}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "73a43b1a-0275-523e-ad37-d72b83b2b870" :question/math-academy-id "q-213296" :question/problem "Given that $10a≡10b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6),$ which of the following statements **must** be true?\n\n- $a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3)$\n- $a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6)$\n- $5a≡5b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,15)$" :question/worked-solution "The division properties of modular arithmetic state the following:\n\n- For $k\\ne 0,$ we have\n\n$$\nka≡kb\\,(\\text{mod}\\,kn)\\,⟺\\,a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\n- For $\\text{gcd}(k,n)=1,$ we have \n\n$$\nka≡kb\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. Applying the first property, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10a & ≡10b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\ 2\\cdot 5\\cdot a & ≡2\\cdot 5\\cdot b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,2\\cdot 3) \\\\ \\cancel{2}\\cdot 5\\cdot a & ≡\\cancel{2}\\cdot 5\\cdot b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,\\cancel{2}\\cdot 3) \\\\ 5a & ≡5b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $gcd(5,3)=1,$ by the second property, we have:\n\n$$\n5a≡5b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3)\\,⟺\\,a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3)\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. By statement I and the first property, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3)\\,⟺\\,5a≡5b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,15)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415041 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152096 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3822141e-3cea-5c34-b925-a522c2273335" :knowledge-point/title "Combining the Division Properties"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "45974aae-697e-59ea-90f3-58a3d7ab07f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414952} {:answer/id #uuid "53ad8ef2-28c6-563f-b134-1a1539671090" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414954} {:answer/id #uuid "8ef2cd18-60ec-5d72-8a48-14b406665bdf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414978} {:answer/id #uuid "9a43e1b2-3043-5dfc-9827-55b5c21ef088" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414981} {:answer/id #uuid "f17a369e-4ccb-56ba-89f8-03b525e4cd5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415010}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "53ad8ef2-28c6-563f-b134-1a1539671090" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414954} :answer-field/id #uuid "27721e63-6adb-57d0-9211-64392779ba7d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415022}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "00e9a03d-cb25-5bd2-bcfe-67d16e1b93d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-213297" :question/problem "Given that $5a≡5b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,15),$ which of the following statements **must** be true?\n\n- $a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6)$\n- $a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3)$\n- $4a≡4b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,12)$" :question/worked-solution "The division properties of modular arithmetic state the following:\n\n- For $k\\ne 0,$ we have\n\n$$\nka≡kb\\,(\\text{mod}\\,kn)\\,⟺\\,a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\n- For $\\text{gcd}(k,n)=1,$ we have \n\n$$\nka≡kb\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. Applying the first property, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5a & ≡5b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,15) \\\\ 5\\cdot a & ≡5\\cdot b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,5\\cdot 3) \\\\ \\cancel{5}\\cdot a & ≡\\cancel{5}\\cdot b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,\\cancel{5}\\cdot 3) \\\\ a & ≡b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. By statement II and the first property, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3)\\,⟺\\,4a≡4b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,12)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\n.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415049 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152096 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3822141e-3cea-5c34-b925-a522c2273335" :knowledge-point/title "Combining the Division Properties"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "411fcf4c-0dfe-5eb4-b277-2957160dccc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414950} {:answer/id #uuid "74b71bad-1c7b-5ec7-82dd-47d6ce2c7ca6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414967} {:answer/id #uuid "81d02ebf-0e9e-55cb-a573-4ae072ffecb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414972} {:answer/id #uuid "dfb5e76a-e012-5b9d-add1-ff7fc168f39c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 175